Lens gives every device on the network an identity that survives an address
change, and hangs everything OPNsense already knows off it.

OPNsense records what traffic happened and, separately, who is on the network
right now. It never joins the two over time: flow history is aggregated on the
source address and nothing else, and device identity exists only in the present
tense. So the answer to "what was that spike last Tuesday" is an IP address.

Lens closes that gap. It reads OPNsense's own data through its APIs rather than
collecting its own, and stores only what nothing else keeps: identity over time,
the operator's own names and tags, and computed baselines.

- Configuration and setup live under Services: Lens
- The views live under Reporting: Lens
- It does not replace Reporting: Insight, Health, NetFlow or Unbound DNS
- It supplies widgets to the dashboard OPNsense already has

Lens reads. The one exception, always triggered by the operator and never on
install, is switching on the data sources it needs.

Plugin Changelog
================

0.2_3

* the harvest is fetched and committed in four hour chunks. On a firewall with
  nineteen interfaces the single request it used to make was large enough to
  lock the database while it was inserted
* a harvest that times out or fails now says so, instead of reporting that it
  found nothing
* a reader that arrives during a write waits instead of reporting the database
  as locked

0.2_2

* the hourly harvest now records which interface a flow crossed. Without it a
  device and the far end of its own connection are indistinguishable, because
  NetFlow writes the destination address into the source field for the outbound
  half of every flow
* schema 2 drops the traffic rows collected without it and refetches whatever
  NetFlow still holds; devices, hostnames and address history are untouched

0.2

* Lens now keeps its own records, in /var/db/lens
* records which device held which address, and when - nothing in OPNsense does
* copies hourly per-device traffic out of NetFlow before OPNsense deletes it
  24 hours after writing it; that history cannot be recovered any other way
* retention, a disk ceiling and a working purge ship with the first row written
* the Data Sources page reports what has been kept, and flags a duty that has
  stopped running

0.1_4

* fixes a bug where the page could report "10 leases from dnsmasq" and "Unbound
  is the only resolver here" at the same time

0.1_3

* a resolver that is simply not the one in use is reported as unavailable, not
  as a fault -- amber is reserved for things worth fixing

0.1_2

* the data source page now judges rather than pings: ready, needs attention or
  unavailable, each with the cause and what would change it
* says which interfaces NetFlow is capturing and, more usefully, which it is not
* reports a resolver that runs but records nothing as unusable, instead of green
* states how long OPNsense keeps each kind of traffic data
* shows what the report itself cost, per call

0.1_1

* first release: package skeleton, menu placement and a data source liveness
  check under Services: Lens and Reporting: Lens
